  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday)\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nMonday currently looks to feature low end (~10-30%) potential for \nrecord-setting heat and mostly dry weather, as the previous upper \nridge remains the dominant regional control of sensible \nconditions. Overnight conditions will become notably warmer by \nMonday night as well, with minimum temperatures +5-10 degrees \n(near 70 degrees) compared to late this week/weekend.\n\nThe pattern looks to turn more active by the time we get to Tuesday \nas Pacific-based systems, including the remnants of Hurricane Simon, \nbegin to shift closer to the area. While our current gridded \nforecast calls for the hottest temperatures of the next week on \nTuesday (mid-90s), the combination of a more muted low-level thermal \nresponse, increasingly cloudy skies and at least low (~20-30%) \npotential for rain/storms casts some doubt on achieving these well \nabove-average conditions. \n\nBetter/more widespread chances for rain and storms look to \nmaterialize by Tuesday night into Wednesday, as a previously closed \nupper system across the Great Basin begins to open and accelerate \neastward towards the Plains. Given a seasonably anomalous moisture \nadvection pattern in advance of this system, and with continued warm \ntemperatures, we will be keeping a close watch on the potential \nfor strong-severe thunderstorms by this time as well.\n\nA more notable frontal passage and associated cooldown looks occur a \nbit later than previously advertised, now focused on Thursday into \nFriday. Depending on the speed/timing of the front, some continued \nrain/storm chance is possible across central and eastern portions of \nthe area into Thursday evening.\n\nUngar",
